Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. 

Bonus - Credit Education:

What Many Repair Companies Don't Understand About The Mortgage Approval
Process

Many of your client's may come to you by way of a realtor, mortgage
professional or simply with the goal of purchasing a home. The content
in today's bonus lesson will give you a clear understanding of you your
work factors into the mortgage loan process. Being equipped with this
information will help you to create a more specific action plan for
clients with the goal of purchasing a home as well as understanding why
some mortgage professionals are reluctant to work with credit repair
(CR) companies. When you begin to understand why, you will know how to
approach skeptical mortgage professionals from a different angle by
speaking their language and hopefully secure more affiliate
partnerships.    

Secrets of the Mortgage Lending Industry

A primary source of income for the bureaus is reselling reports for
potential real estate purchases. The bureaus typically do not directly
sell reports to banks, loan officers, or mortgage brokers. Instead,
resellers contract with the credit bureaus to provide a compilation of
data from all three bureaus, commonly known as a Trimerge Report. This
is all good on the face of it, except for the fact that trimerge reports
tend to have the highest amount of errors and much lower data matching
requirements than a consumer report. When a potential home owner gets
their mortgage reports pulled by a mortgage professional, they find out
that there are many negative items on their credit report that did not
show up when they pulled the consumer version of the report. As a
result, the consumer is either improperly denied a loan, or is forced to
pay a higher interest rate.

The resellers are contracted with the bureaus and as part of their
contract with banks, loan officers, and mortgage brokers, they often
include verbiage in their contract that restricts the banks, loan
officers or mortgage brokers from providing or referring any consumer to
a credit repair company, or assisting a consumer to repair their credit.
The language is so restrictive that a bank, loan officer, or mortgage
broker could permanently lose their ability to resell credit reports
from that reseller or in general if they help a consumer legally repair
their credit, or refer them to a legal credit repair business. Equally
important, the trimege report available to the mortgage professional is
prohibited from being used for any other purpose other than to determine
the consumer's qualification for a mortgage loan. This report can not be
used to conduct credit repair under any circumstance. If it is
determined that a mortgage professional has abused their reseller
privileges by using these reports themselves or by any of their
affiliates to repair client files, their resell privileges are
permanently revoked.  

It is absolutely true, if you've ever heard the credit bureaus do not
want consumers to repair their credit and don't want qualified
licensed people (the mortgage lenders) advising on credit repair. The
worse off the consumer is in this area, the more money the bureaus make.

The bureaus do allow for one type of consumer defense to the trimerge
trap called rapid rescoring, where the reseller typically contests just
a few well-chosen items for a fee of $30 to $90 per item. They usually
achieve resolution within 72 hours. The cost for the repairs then become
part of the closing costs and may result in saving thousands of dollars
over the course of a loan. So, the resellers are allowed to sell
ancillary products to loan officers, bankers, mortgage lenders to
analyze scores and they provide rapid rescoring processes.

But, Bruce, Just Wait One Minute! Seventy-two-hour resolution???
The consumer and many legitimate credit repair companies are unable to
get an answer within 30 days, but the resellers, who enable to CRAs to
make millions of dollars, can get responses within 72 hours. Sounds like
the reporting agencies have found yet another way to profit from their
own incompetence! The reporting agencies should be paying the consumers
because they had errors; they shouldn't be able to profit from their
errors. So, as you can see, the bureaus' profits increase as the
consumers score decreases! 

It's also much clearer now why the bureaus don't like us: they
don't want the competition!

Let us be clear: the situation is highly unethical and should be
illegal. The bureaus and resellers are contractually forcing the
mortgage broker/lender/banker into performing malpractice. The
banker/lender has a fiduciary responsibility to the consumer, but they
neglect to fulfill this responsibility because they are in fear of
losing their contract with their reseller. They should be helping
consumers in any way possible, either by referral to a qualified credit
specialist, or by assisting the consumer on their own. Due to
contractual restrictions, they withhold from referring or providing a
service that consumers need. With the wide acceptance and knowledge of
the significant amount of credit errors that exist on most credit
reports, forcing professionals to withhold referrals or advice by
threatening to take away their livelihood (credit pulling privileges)
should be considered a crime.

What Repair Specialists Need to Know and Do Before Advising Their Client
To Apply For a Home Loan...

When a consumer legally disputes an item on their report, the Fair
Credit Reporting Act mandates that the data furnisher mark the item as
"in dispute." This way if someone is applying for credit, the potential
new creditor will be warned that the consumer is disputing the item, and
they can make their underwriting judgment call based on the information
provide. Due to the automation of the banking system, underwriting
systems, and approval systems, it is rare for an actual underwriter to
make a decision. As with so many of our everyday processes, a computer
actually makes the underwriting decision to approve you for a loan.

This automated review process is programmed to automatically deny any
loan file of a consumer who has any items noted as "in dispute" on their
credit reports. Therefore, while the federal government and the FTC
publicly support consumer rights and pass legislation such as the FCRA,
their actions deny consumers fundamental rights that are afforded for in
the FCRA. This once again leaves consumers alone, injured, frustrated,
and desperate to the point of taking extraordinary measures. 

Despite the consumer protection laws that empower consumers with the
ability to dispute, doing so locks them out of government mortgage
programs, as the automated underwriting system will not accept credit
files with items in dispute. If the consumer does dispute an item, the
data furnishers have a method of payback for consumers exercising their
rights.

Even after a disputed item's investigation is complete, the data
furnishers continue to report the dispute as active. Let's say you're
disputing items for a client and the results have come back with some
items being verified and still remaining on the report; however, you and
your client decide that the score is reasonable enough to proceed with
the process. Well, even though the investigation is complete and the
accounts may be marked as "account meets requirements of the FCRA" the
data furnishers still continues to report the items as "in dispute,"
which will still trigger an automated denial of your client's mortgage
application even after the investigation is complete.

What do you do now? Many will advise just contact the bureaus to take
the items out of dispute. Let's say you decide to dispute the "in
dispute" verbiage with the bureaus...Well, you do this by opening a new
dispute! This means that the item is still in dispute, and the automated
denial is still in play. 

What many mortgage professionals know but many repair specialist do not
is that the solution to this issue is going directly to the data
furnisher to have them change the status on the report, not the credit
bureaus. But the fact is, that is more aggravation than you or your
client, or in the language of the law - consumers should be put through.

Understanding the restrictions that are placed on resellers of credit
reports and the lack of knowledge of many repair specialist when
handling the file of clients looking to purchase a home gives insight
into why some mortgage professionals may be reluctant to work with
repair companies. Hopefully the information we've covered can give you
some pointers in speaking the language of the mortgage professional and
wowing  them by being able to tell them exactly how you get your
client's to pass through underwriting once their repair process is
complete.  

Now,Bruce, Go Be The Expert!

