ass=3D"">Secrets of the Mortgage Lending Industry<br></span><br class=3D"">=
</div>=0A<span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 15px; font-weight: inher=
it; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">A primary s=
ource of income for the bureaus is reselling reports for potential real est=
ate purchases. The bureaus typically do not directly sell reports to banks,=
 loan officers, or mortgage brokers. Instead, resellers contract with the c=
redit bureaus to provide a compilation of data from all three bureaus, comm=
only known as a Trimerge Report. This is all good on the face of it, except=
 for the fact that trimerge reports tend to have the highest amount of erro=
rs and much lower data matching requirements than a consumer report. When a=
 potential home owner gets their mortgage reports pulled by a mortgage prof=
essional, they find out that there are many negative items on their credit =
report that did not show up when they pulled the consumer version of the re=
port. As a result, the consumer is either improperly denied a loan, or is f=
orced to pay a=0Ahigher interest rate.<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">The res=
ellers are contracted with the bureaus and as part of their contract with b=
anks, loan officers, and mortgage brokers, they often include verbiage in t=
heir contract that restricts the banks, loan officers or mortgage brokers f=
rom providing or referring any consumer to a credit repair company, or assi=
sting a consumer to repair their credit. The language is so restrictive tha=
t a bank, loan officer, or mortgage broker could permanently lose their abi=
lity to resell credit reports from that reseller or in general if they help=
 a consumer legally repair their credit, or refer them to a legal credit re=
pair business. Equally important, the trimege report available to the mortg=
age professional is prohibited from being used for any other purpose other =
than to determine the consumer's qualification for a mortgage loan. This re=
port can not be used to conduct credit repair under any circumstance. If it=
 is determined that a=0Amortgage professional has abused their reseller pri=
vileges by using these reports themselves or by any of their affiliates to =
repair client files, their resell privileges are permanently revoked.=C2=
=A0=C2=A0<br class=3D""><br class=3D"">It is absolutely true, if you've eve=
r heard the credit bureaus do not want consumers to repair their credit and=
 don=E2=80=99t want qualified licensed people (the mortgage lenders) advisi=
ng on credit repair. The worse off the consumer is in this area, the more m=
oney the bureaus make.<br class=3D""><br>The bureaus do allow for one type =
of consumer defense to the trimerge trap called rapid rescoring, where the =
reseller typically contests just a few well-chosen items for a fee of $30 t=
o $90 per item. They usually achieve resolution within 72 hours. The cost f=
or the repairs then become part of the closing costs and may result in savi=
ng thousands of dollars over the course of a loan. So, the resellers are al=
lowed to sell ancillary products to loan officers,=0Abankers, mortgage lend=
ers to analyze scores and they provide rapid rescoring processes.<br class=
=3D""><br>But, Bruce, Just Wait One Minute! <span style=3D"color: inherit; =
font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoratio=
n: inherit;" class=3D"">Seventy-two-hour resolution???</span> The consumer =
and many legitimate credit repair companies are unable to get an answer wit=
hin 30 days, but the resellers, who enable to CRAs to make millions of doll=
ars, can get responses within 72 hours. Sounds like the reporting agencies =
have found yet another way to profit from their own incompetence! The repor=
ting agencies should be paying the consumers because they had errors; they =
shouldn=E2=80=99t be able to profit from their errors. So, as you can see, =
the bureaus=E2=80=99 profits increase as the consumers score decreases! <br=
><br>It=E2=80=99s also much clearer now why the bureaus don=E2=80=99t like =
us: they don=E2=80=99t want the competition!<br class=3D""><br>Let us be cl=
ear: the=0Asituation is highly unethical and should be illegal. The bureaus=
 and resellers are contractually forcing the mortgage broker/lender/banker =
into performing malpractice. The banker/lender has a fiduciary responsibili=
ty to the consumer, but they neglect to fulfill this responsibility because=
 they are in fear of losing their contract with their reseller. They should=
 be helping consumers in any way possible, either by referral to a qualifie=
d credit specialist, or by assisting the consumer on their own. Due to cont=
ractual restrictions, they withhold from referring or providing a service t=
hat consumers need. With the wide acceptance and knowledge of the significa=
nt amount of credit errors that exist on most credit reports, forcing profe=
ssionals to withhold referrals or advice by threatening to take away their =
livelihood (credit pulling privileges) should be considered a crime.<br><br=
></span><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-align: cen=
ter; font-size:=0A20px;"><span style=3D"color: #000000; font-size: inherit;=
 font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=
=3D"">What Repair Specialists Need to Know and Do <span style=3D"color: #ff=
0000; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-=
decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">Before </span>Advising Their Client To App=
ly For a Home Loan...</span></div>=0A<span style=3D"color: inherit; font-si=
ze: 15px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inhe=
rit;" class=3D""><br>When a consumer legally disputes an item on their repo=
rt, the Fair Credit Reporting Act mandates that the data furnisher mark the=
 item as "<span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-weight: b=
old; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">in dispute=
</span>." This way if someone is applying for credit, the potential new cre=
ditor will be warned that the consumer is disputing the item, and they can =
make their underwriting judgment call based on the information provide. Due=
 to the automation of the banking system, underwriting systems, and approva=
l systems, it is rare for an actual underwriter to make a decision. As with=
 so many of our everyday processes, a computer actually makes the underwrit=
ing decision to approve you for a loan.<br><br>This automated review proces=
s is programmed to automatically deny any loan=0Afile of a consumer who has=
 any items noted as "in dispute" on their credit reports. Therefore, while =
the federal government and the FTC publicly support consumer rights and pas=
s legislation such as the FCRA, their actions deny consumers fundamental ri=
ghts that are afforded for in the FCRA. This once again leaves consumers al=
one, injured, frustrated, and desperate to the point of taking extraordinar=
y measures. <br><br>Despite the consumer protection laws that empower consu=
mers with the ability to dispute, doing so locks them out of government mor=
tgage programs, as the automated underwriting system will not accept credit=
 files with items in dispute. If the consumer does dispute an item, the dat=
a furnishers have a method of payback for consumers exercising their rights=
.<br><br>Even after a disputed item's investigation is complete, the data f=
urnishers continue to report the dispute as active. Let=E2=80=99s say you'r=
e disputing items for a client and the results have come=0Aback with some i=
tems being verified and still remaining on the report; however, you and you=
r client decide that the score is reasonable enough to proceed with the pro=
cess. Well, even though the investigation is complete and the accounts may =
be marked as "account meets requirements of the FCRA" the data furnishers s=
till continues to report the items as "in dispute," which will still trigge=
r an automated denial of your client's mortgage application even after the =
investigation is complete.<br><br>What do you do now? Many will advise just=
 contact the bureaus to take the items out of dispute. Let's say you decide=
 to dispute the "in dispute" verbiage with the bureaus...Well, you do this =
by opening a new dispute! This means that the item is still in dispute, and=
 the automated denial is still in play. <br><br>What many mortgage professi=
onals know but many repair specialist do not is that the solution to this i=
ssue is going directly to the data furnisher to have them change=0Athe stat=
us on the report, not the credit bureaus. But the fact is, that is more agg=
ravation than you or your client, or in the language of the law - consumers=
 should be put through. <br><br>Understanding the restrictions that are pla=
ced on resellers of credit reports and the lack of knowledge of many repair=
 specialist when handling the file of clients looking to purchase a home gi=
ves insight into why some mortgage professionals may be reluctant to work w=
ith repair companies. Hopefully the information we've covered can give you =
some pointers in speaking the language of the mortgage professional and wow=
ing=C2=A0 them by being able to tell them exactly how you get your client's=
 to pass through underwriting once their repair process is complete.=C2=
